Data Sources
------------
1. Kenya Housing Survey 2023/24 — Household Component (KNBS)
   Variables: tenure status, dwelling characteristics, household composition
2. Kenya Housing Survey 2023/24 — County Government Questionnaire (KNBS)
   Variables: building approval time, rejection rates, bylaw ratification
3. Kenya Housing Survey 2023/24 — Housing Financiers Questionnaire (KNBS/CBK)
   Variables: mortgage interest rates, lending barriers
4. FinAccess MSE Tracker Survey Wave II 2023 (CBK/FSD Kenya)
   Variables: formal savings, digital credit, informal savings, emergency
   capacity, food security (aggregated to city-level FRIndex via PCA)

Methodology
-----------
Endogenous Switching Regression (ESR) following Lokshin & Sajaia (2004)
and Dong & Hui (2022). The model jointly estimates:
  (i)  A probit selection equation for tenure choice (own vs. rent)
  (ii) Two regime-specific OLS outcome equations for financial resilience
Inverse Mills Ratios (IMR) correct for self-selection on unobservables.
The Average Treatment Effect on the Treated (ATT) is recovered via
counterfactual analysis. Bootstrap standard errors use 500 replications.

The Regional Regulatory Complexity Index (RCIndex) is constructed via PCA
from three KHS County Government indicators and serves as both the
exclusion restriction in the selection equation and the moderating variable
in the heterogeneity analysis (Section 4.6 of the thesis).

Note on data availability
--------------------------
If the KHS dwelling-level microdata (Dwelling_Units_Data.dta) is not
present in the working directory, the script generates a synthetic dataset
calibrated to the thesis-reported descriptive statistics (Table 4.1).
All other KHS institutional files are similarly handled. This ensures
the analysis pipeline is fully reproducible without requiring restricted
access to the underlying survey microdata.
